{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"taurine-smiling-hamadryad","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Summary\n\nThe MCP endpoint became unreachable on 2026-08-28 (other sessions reported \"rebar's MCP is\ndown\"). The container was healthy — a local probe to `127.0.0.1:8092/health` returned `200` in\n1.8ms — but external requests to `https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/mcp` timed out. Five\nprobes sent ~12s apart were all logged by the server within the SAME millisecond, i.e. queued\nand flushed in a burst. nginx was buffering to `/var/lib/nginx/tmp/` on a root disk at **92%\n(2.6G free)**.\n\nReclaiming docker garbage took the disk to **57% (13G free)** and the endpoint recovered\nimmediately: six consecutive external probes returned `401` in ~250ms.\n\n## Why the automatic reclaim did not self-heal it\n\n`prune_docker_caches()` (`infra/scripts/autodeploy.sh:276-284`) runs exactly two commands, and\nneither could reclaim what had accumulated:\n\n    timeout 120 docker builder prune -f --keep-storage \"$BUILD_CACHE_KEEP\"   # BUILD_CACHE_KEEP=5GB\n    timeout 120 docker image prune -f                                        # no -a\n\n1. **The build cache was capped at exactly what was filling the disk.** `BUILD_CACHE_KEEP`\n   defaults to `5GB` (`:124`) and the cache measured **5.254GB**. The prune was working as\n   designed and retaining it.\n2. **`docker image prune -f` without `-a` removes only DANGLING images.** The comment at `:272`\n   states the intent: *\"TAGGED images are never touched (:prev is the rollback lifeline)\"*. But\n   blue-green tags every build (`rebar-mcp-<sha>`), so the box held **44 images / 11.09GB, of\n   which 10.08GB was reclaimable**. Tagged-image accumulation is the dominant leak and nothing\n   is permitted to remove it.\n\nSo the gate ran on schedule, logged `\"disk pressure reclaim complete\"`, freed ~nothing, then\nthrottled for `PRESSURE_PRUNE_MIN_INTERVAL` (600s) and repeated that misleading success.\n\n`infra/scripts/observability.sh:330-335` says the marker exists so an incident sweep can\n*\"distinguish 'the reclaim gate never ran' from 'it ran and reclaimed nothing' without host\naccess\"*. It cannot: it counts INVOCATIONS, not bytes freed, so both cases look identical.\n\n## Alerting was not the gap — recovery was\n\n`rebar-root-disk-pressure` (threshold 85%, SNS `rebar-gerrit-alerts`) **did fire**: ALARM at\n2026-08-27T23:48 PDT, cleared 2026-08-28T10:45 PDT only when the manual reclaim landed —\n**~11 hours in ALARM**. It flapped beforehand (22:29 / 22:51 / 23:09 / 23:48) as the ineffective\nreclaim freed a little and the disk refilled. A threshold alarm cannot say \"reclaim is running\nand achieving nothing\", which is the actionable condition.\n\n## Scope (operator-directed, 2026-08-28)\n\n1. **Retire the tagged image once its container is fully drained and the cutover is complete.**\n   The blue-green retire path already reaps exited containers; removing the corresponding\n   `rebar-mcp-<sha>` tag belongs there — after the reap, never while serving. Keep the LIVE\n   image and `:prev` as the rollback lifeline.\n2. **Log free space before and after every reclaim**, so effect is observable rather than\n   inferred.\n3. **Emit a loud, distinct ALARM marker when pressure persists across 3 or more reclaim\n   cycles** — the \"reclaim is ineffective\" signal that no existing alarm expresses.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] After a blue-green container is reaped, its `rebar-mcp-<sha>` image is removed, while the\n      live image and `:prev` are retained (checked by a test that reaps a fake container and\n      asserts exactly which tags survive).\n- [ ] A serving container's image is NEVER removed — the retire path removes an image only\n      after the reap for that container has succeeded (checked by a defect-seeded test).\n- [ ] `prune_docker_caches` logs root-disk free space BEFORE and AFTER, and the delta, on every\n      invocation (checked by asserting both values appear in the emitted log line).\n- [ ] When a reclaim completes and the disk is STILL at or above `DISK_PRESSURE_PCT`, a\n      persistent-pressure counter increments; it resets to zero once the disk falls below the\n      threshold (checked by driving the counter across a pressure/relief sequence).\n- [ ] At 3 or more consecutive ineffective cycles, a distinct marker is emitted (not the\n      existing `AUTODEPLOY_DISK_PRESSURE`, which counts invocations) and `observability.sh`\n      publishes it, so it is alarmable without host access.\n- [ ] The new marker fires only on PERSISTENCE: a single pressured cycle that then recovers does\n      NOT emit it (checked by a negative-control test — this is the discriminator, since an\n      alarm that fires on any pressure is the flapping alarm we already have).\n\n## Evidence\n\nDisk 92%→57%; images 44→4 (11.09GB→2.779GB); build cache 5.254GB→0. Endpoint went from four\n12s timeouts to six 401s at ~250ms. Volumes were deliberately untouched (11 present, 1 active).","id":"9bc0-1200-1451-44bb","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":["infra","autodeploy","observability","incident"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Disk-pressure reclaim frees nothing: tagged blue-green images are never retired, and the reclaim reports success without measuring effect"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787940529753431120,"uuid":"1c52814e-0b6f-40f5-95e8-dacbf6638ebd"}